  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light to moderate morning rain still expected. \nThere is a Slight Risk (level 2/5) for severe weather with afternoon \nthunderstorms. A few brief tornadoes and strong wind possible. \n\nEarly morning sfc analysis indicates the remnant sfc low from Isaias \nand the attending UL low situated over TN and a warm front extending \neast from it into the Carolinas. This warm front is forecast to lift \nnorth into the local FA this morning, then the low will cross from \nSW VA toward the Eastern Shore during the afternoon. High pressure \nridging off the New England coast is ridging down the mountains, \nforming a subtle CAD feature over the western piedmont and \nAppalachia. Latest radar shows light to occasionally moderate rain \nacross much of the area and is most widespread around the Richmond \nmetro and the northwestern counties. Winds are gusty at the coast \nahead of the aforementioned features with gusts up to 30mph already \nobserved. High-res guidance does still indicate that rain should \nfill in across the area, becoming moderate to locally heavy at times \nas the warm front pushes into the area. Once this batch of rain \nlifts north, there should be a break in the rain for the middle \nportion of the day. A second round of precip will then cross through \nthe area SW to NE as the low passes through. Total QPF from both \nrounds of rain looks to be quite a bit lower than was guidance \ndepicted several day ago, partially due to the dry slot that was not \noriginally shown. Based on radar trends so far and latest runs of \nthe HREF/REFS, total QPF will likely only be 0.5\" to 1\" at most for \nmuch of the area. Areas along the immediate coast in NC/Virginia \nBeach and far western areas in Louisa/Fluvanna could see closer to \n1.5\". Probs for 2\" have dropped to ~20% for these areas with <10% \nfor the rest of the area. \n\nThe second round of rain this afternoon is where the severe weather \nrisk comes in. CAMs depict a broken line and/or supercells \nprogressing from SW to NE during the late afternoon as the sfc low \ntrek NE and the UL trough pivots into the region. A moist and \nunstable airmass will advect into the area behind the warm front. \n00z guidance suggests MLCAPE will climb into the 1000-1200 J/kg \nrange which is more than enough to support convective activity. Deep \nlayer shear of 40kt+ along with low level shear of 30kt+ and long, \ncurved hodographs further supports the threat. Add to that SRH \nvalues over 200, low LCLs, and SE sfc winds and you've got yourself \na decent set up for a few tornadoes and damaging wind gusts. The SPC \noutlook for today is mostly unchanged from yesterday with a slight \nrisk for interior portions of the FA and a Marginal Risk along the \ncoast. Highest confidence is south of I-64 since CAD may dampen \ninstability across the north and northwest. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms from this second round should taper off by \n8pm. Additional showers on the backside of the low will be possible \nlate tonight into early Monday, primarily north of I-64 and on the \nEastern Shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief warmup is expected early next week with \ncooler temperatures later in the week.\n\nAn upper level ridge centered across the central CONUS \ngradually builds into the region through the week. Meanwhile, \nsurface high pressure remains centered across the Deep South \nthrough midweek before a second area of high pressure builds in \nfrom the north late in the week. This will bring a warmup with \nhighs in the 70s for most Mon, lower 80s for most Tue (mid- \nupper 70s across the Eastern Shore), and mid 80s for most Wed \n(upper 70s to near 80F for the Eastern Shore). Temps gradually \ncool late in the week but remain at or above average through \nFri. Otherwise, mainly dry conditions are expected from Mon- \nThu.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Coastal Flood Advisories continue for portions of \neast-central VA along the Bay and tidal Rappahannock/Potomac for \nminor flooding today. Coastal Flood Statements continue for Hampton \nRoads, the tidal York/James, and the bayside of the lower Maryland \nand VA eastern shore for nuisance flooding. A high Surf Advisory has \nbeen issued for all coastal zone for ~8 ft nearshore waves. \n\nA rapid rise in water levels is expected with the high tide cycle \ntoday, occurring mainly in the morning for the lower Chesapeake Bay, \nlower James, and during the afternoon farther north across the \nmiddle Peninsula/northern Neck and late afternoon into early evening \nfor the lower Maryland eastern shore Bay zones. Given the E-SE wind \ndirection, the highest impacts are expected across areas in the \nAdvisory (east central VA along the tidal Rappahannock/Potomac and \nwest shore of the Bay. This should be just one tide cycle with the \nfast moving nature of this system and winds becoming S-SW later this \nafternoon and tonight.",
